{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1506778-armor-and-durability","id":1506778,"title":"Armor and durability?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Werewolf: The Apocalypse"],"posts":[{"date":"2023-03-28T12:45:40-07:00","id":1506778,"title":"Armor and durability?","content":"How do you guys tackle armor and it's durability?<br /> <br /> If someone has +3 armor on and go into combat, and let's say go up against a Spiral who uses their claws, you'd assume the armor would take major damage and break, correct? As a Storyteller, does that come into play, or do we assume the armor is perfectly fine after going toe to toe with monsters and taking on damage?<br /> <br /> Thanks!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23998-dynamiterave","name":"DynamiteRave","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"16"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-28T12:51:58-07:00","id":1506780,"title":"","content":"Out of the box, the system doesnt have a hard definition how durable the armor would be, so it's down to the Storyteller to figure out what makes sense to them.<br /> <br /> You could go with armor taking say 2x damage of the soak before it's useless/needs repairs if that's your jive. I honestly wouldn't bother with it, though - especially as many forms of armor are magical or inborn to the critters of Werewolf.<br /> <br /> If the concern is from a balance standpoint, remember that called shots, attacks that ignore armor or Fur Gnarl are always available.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-28T14:33:35-07:00","id":1506789,"title":"","content":"Well... armor also just kinda sucks in the game. Unless you have supernaturally enhanced armor (which I think most players are going to expect is supernaturally tough for the process of getting it), you're not getting that much out of it in general. Werewolves even less so.<br /> <br /> The few times we've had armor wearing Garou in our games that weren't using Fetish armor, we just said the armor was destroyed after the fight if you took (rating + 1) L or A damage during the fight. Not super realistic, but simple to keep it fun and things moving.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-28T16:00:53-07:00","id":1506799,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Asmodai</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1506778-armor-and-durability?p=1506780#post1506780\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Out of the box, the system doesnt have a hard definition how durable the armor would be...</div> </div> </div> </div>Not quite. Fur Gnarl has one built into its mechanics, even if it might not be the best.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23-saur-ops-specialist","name":"Saur Ops Specialist","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar23_3.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9371"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-28T17:00:33-07:00","id":1506805,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Saur Ops Specialist</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-apocalypse-aa/1506778-armor-and-durability?p=1506799#post1506799\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Not quite. Fur Gnarl has one built into its mechanics, even if it might not be the best.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, I noted it. But it doesn't say anything about repairs or mechanical impact on armor, as an item. It just lowers soak and it works on anything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:arial\">What doesn't kill you, makes you...<i> stranger</i>.</span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1182-asmodai","name":"Asmodai","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1182_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2989"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-30T03:14:23-07:00","id":1506936,"title":"","content":"I agree with <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar617_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"617\" data-vbnamecard=\"617\">Heavy Arms</a> on this one, armor aren't that usefull considering the increasing dex penalty with it's resistance, especially when a single attack from supernatural beings (werewolves in this case) can overcome it with their monstruous strength and their claws.<br /> <br /> Enhance them by supernatural means correct a little but still, in real life when you take a shot from a gun a bulletproof vest stop the bullet and the wearer is more or less injured but with bashing kind of damage unless the weapon were powerfull enough to pierce it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26839-cain-loup-noir","name":"Cain Loup-Noir","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26839_1.jpg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"390"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-30T05:58:31-07:00","id":1506942,"title":"","content":"<br /> depends on the material. Simplifying an extreme IRL example: there's three main types of armour plates designed to deal with rifles: Ceramic, which breaks in around the shot for maximum absorption so it's definitely going to do nothing if you get shot a second time in the impacted area; steel, which just blocks the shot with some small amount of deformation/absorption going on; and plastic fibres, I forgot how that one functioned, but it's by far the least common method. Steel, given enough, can be shot hundreds of times and still save your life. Ceramic has it's advantages, but it wouldn't be especially egregious to treat it like a consumable item. Everything about the Crinos form is magic, so how those claws slash through steel is up to you.<br /> <br /> I have done my own research on putting armour into the WoD system in a way that feels simulative enough. Here's my take.<br /> <br /> If you double the dice armour gives you for the kinds of things that armour is designed to protect from, you get a reasonably sound system that isn't overly cumbersome. But it does kinda need you to know what armour is supposed to protect you from (IE a balistic vest isn't a stab vest, and Kevlar will do great against pistols and shotguns but not rifles)<br /> <br /> IRL very few armours have actually restricted the mobility of a healthy adult to the extent of what could be quantified as a whole die of DEX penalty, nevermind two of three die. Something crazy like an EOD suit or Tournament armour might knock your dex down significantly, but they aren't designed for ordinary combat. Rather than a dex penalty, I'd consider the following: <br /> <br /> <br /> - Wearing armour is suspicious outside of an active warzone.<br /> - Helmets are a problem for perception.<br /> - Comfort and fatigue. Concealable armour is usually more uncomfortable, and while I don't think I would be much slowed down in a sprint wearing full plate I don't much fancy a half-marathon in one or spending half a day in it. Being tired should give you penalties. People generally wear as little armour as they can get away with.<br /> Normal soldiers wear heavier armour on their torso compared to their limbs. Like your average geared up US infantry combating insurgents would wear kevlar (in game this'd be class 3) on their limbs but ceramic plates on their body (class 6) . Kevlar is good enough for bombs but it's just too much to try and protect the limbs from rifles. While ceramic is fairly light going into battle as the Michelin man has it's own problems. If we just discuss metal armour for melee the torso historically had thicker metal than the rest of the body, so the numbers for a knight in the 14th century aren't going to be significantly different. Armour is a good method to get players to start calling shots and thinking more tactically in combat.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":25794,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","user":"MyWifeIsScary","date":". 03-30-2023, 09:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/25794-mywifeisscary","name":"MyWifeIsScary","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar25794_2.png","title":"Banned","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2019"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3711"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2023-03-30T14:04:27-07:00","id":1506989,"title":"","content":"I think if I were inclined to do a more thorough house rule on armor in the WoD ruleset, I'd look at the CofD for a few reasons:<br /> <br /> 1) The CofD has standardized rules for item durability. Even if the CofD doesn't track wear and tear in combat, there's a least a system for how much &quot;health&quot; items have, when they start losing function, etc. if you want that level of detail.<br /> <br /> 2) The CofD 2e specifically gives armor two values. Armor's first number is how much damage it reduces (weapon damage bonuses and armor reductions in CofD 2e are raw numbers, not dice), with a caveat that mundane armors can't reduce damage to zero, and if they would the target still takes 1B. The second value is a damage type reduction, though CofD 2e focuses on firearms specifically. So if you're wearing a Kevlar vest that's rated 1/3, you reduce three lethal damage from guns to bashing damage, and reduce the total damage inflicted by 1 (starting with the worst type of damage). So if you're shot for 5 L damage, you take 1 L and 3 B. A considerable increase in survival, but without supernatural healing powers, that's going to add up fast.<br /> <br /> 3) While that's plenty of increased granularity for most games set in modern nights outside an active war zone, like I said in #1, you can easily build up from there. You could, for example, give the damage type score tags for different kinds of attacks. Something that's good for keeping you from dying from gunshots isn't necessarily going to do much against a sword or a claw, and vice versa, even if the amount of total force reduction would be a same. A prison CO's stab vest could reduce blades instead of bullets as the plastic they use in those is meant to stop shivs not guns, but still basically have the same rating as a Kevlar vest. Adding trauma plates to a basic vest is also something you could add to the system fairly easily compared to the WoD rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/617-heavy-arms","name":"Heavy Arms","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar617_2.jpeg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"12634"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}